2026-11-01:

- tests: 801 unit tests (from 642), valgrind clean. New: charbuf, ucs,
  textbuf, atomic, cdb, critbit, textcode codecs, stralloc, strarray, strlist
  and the buffer file/mmap/gzip functions; 76 empty placeholder tests were
  filled with real checks (37 remain, listed by `grep -n "TEST(.*) {}"`).
- fixed (found by those tests): stralloc_trunc left the capacity field wrong so
  the next append overflowed the heap; stralloc_free kept len and a;
  stralloc_copywcb and catwcb counted wrongly and cut multi-byte characters;
  stralloc_shrink failed on an empty string; stralloc_expand did nothing off
  Windows (now expands $NAME and ${NAME}); stralloc_scan looped forever on a
  scan function that consumes nothing; fmt_uuencoded measured too little and
  read past its input; fmt_base64url measured one byte too much;
  fmt_stripwhitespace crashed on a NULL destination and kept a trailing blank;
  fmt_tofrom_array failed on an empty target; ucs_from_latin1_char mapped every
  character from 0x80 to U+0000 and str_utf8_latin1 never ended on invalid
  UTF-8; scan_ansiskip read beyond its limit; str_versdiff said 1 for equal
  numbers; charbuf_pred_skip lost its count at the end of input;
  strarray_unshift and unshiftn stored the caller's pointers and strarray_free
  then freed them (tinyproxy, exe-wrap); strarray_splice, union, intersection,
  transform and copy were wrong or crashed; strarray_glob returned glob's
  error code as a match count and lib/glob.h shadowed the system header,
  corrupting the stack; strlist_pos measured every element like the first,
  which broke strlist_slice; strlist_removeb, remove_at, pop, sub, remove_all
  and splice left empty elements behind or did nothing; buffer constructors
  left cookie uninitialised (is_textbuf read garbage); buffer_munmap and
  buffer_free left a length behind; buffer_mmapshared_fd now owns its
  descriptor like buffer_mmapread_fd.
- fixed (found by later tests): ihex_record_write wrote a wrong checksum and
  ihex_record_insert mishandled overlapping records; the playlist readers were
  rewritten (playlist_free and playlist_state_new are new); xml_delete used
  freed memory and xml_find_element_attr, xml_path and xml_get_text(NULL)
  were wrong; cpp tokens created at the beginning of a line lost that flag;
  scan_ip6if freed an invalid pointer; json_parse_number failed on a number at
  the end of the input and json_property_set_str printed a debug line.
  ihex_strip_below, str_str, taia_fmtfrac, mmap_anon and buffer_mmapshared
  are new; declarations were added for byte_case_start, str_versdiff,
  json_todouble, fd_filesize, buffer_fromstr, io_dup and path_canonicalize.
- fixed (found by those tests): buffer_bzip, buffer_bunzip and their _fd forms
  were not compiled at all and buffer_bunzip had no read operation; the brotli
  buffer was rewritten (it overran its output and failed on larger data);
  iom_wait never returned a queued event, iom_add could not re-arm a
  descriptor that had fired; byte_camelize read one byte beyond a trailing
  dash; buffer_put_quoted dropped control characters into bare octal digits
  (now backslash and three digits). Declarations and tests for the last
  placeholders: 799 unit tests, none of them empty except where a function
  needs a feature this build lacks.
- API: renamed the files slist_find_remove, dlist_find_remove, str_utf8_latin,
  xml_set_attribute_long and socket_v6loopback to the name of the function they
  define; added str_str, taia_fmtfrac, mmap_anon, buffer_mmapshared and the
  fmt_to_sa2 family, which were declared and never defined; removed
  declarations nobody implemented (fmt_base85, scan_base85, array_shift,
  array_chop, io_queueforread, io_queueforwrite, io_starteventloopthread,
  json_parse_null_or_undefined, cpp_define_gnu_macros, the variadic
  xml_find_pred and xml_find_all) and the unused headers linked_list.h, tree.h
  and memory.h.
- genmakefile: removed the discontinued HI-TECH C compiler type (htc, picc,
  picc18); XC8 v1.x, XC8 v2+, SDCC and the others stay. The MPLAB 8 output
  knows the xc8 and sdcc suites only.
- doc: added doc/genmakefile-toolchains.md (flags, quirks and option maps of
  XC8 v1/v2, SDCC, MCC18, gputils, MPLAB X and MPLAB 8, read from the build
  systems of the PIC repositories); the genmakefile specification is complete
  with firmware inference, MPLAB X import, verification plan and roadmap;
  draft descriptors in src/genmakefile/toolchains/.
- libowfat compatibility: checked every prototype of libowfat 0.32 against
  lib/*.h and ran a differential test of 94 fmt, scan, str, byte, case and
  uintN functions against the real libowfat (tests/libowfat-compat, see
  doc/libowfat-compat.md). Fixed the places where a program written for
  libowfat no longer compiled or behaved differently: buffer_get_token_pred
  and the stralloc token predicates, buffer_init taking a function pointer
  type, io_pipe, io_socketpair and friends writing int64 descriptors, the
  scan_*longlong family taking unsigned long long pointers (scan_u64,
  scan_x64, scan_i64 are the uint64 versions), fmt_escapecharjson,
  rangecheck.h assign(dest, src), scan_varint (truncated values above 32
  bits and accepted non-minimal encodings), scan_asn1derlength (read an
  uninitialised length), scan_fromhex (returned garbage for non-hex
  characters), str_diff (compared signed bytes), fmt_str, fmt_httpdate,
  fmt_iso8601 and fmt_escapecharxml (wrote a terminating NUL byte beyond the
  returned length), fmt_utf8 (refused values above 0x3ffffff) and
  fmt_escapecharc (refused characters above 0xff).
- doc: added doc/small-and-fast.md (the design rules of "Writing Small And
  Fast Software" and where they are in the tree, dietlibc support) and
  doc/libowfat-compat.md (what libowfat compatibility means, how it is
  tested, the differences that stay); README.txt starts with a primer on
  the lineage djb, libowfat, c-utils.
- cdb, critbit: added the constant database (cdb.h, cdb_make.h) and the
  crit-bit tree of libowfat (critbit.h) as modules; added the libowfat
  functions fmt_netstring, fmt_varint, fmt_pb_*, scan_netstring, scan_pb_*,
  scan_iso8601, scan_8longlong, fmt_copybytes, fmt_escapecharhtml,
  mmap_readat, EDISCON and EPROCLIM, the headers compiletimeassert.h,
  isset.h, va_narg.h, readclose.h, openreadclose.h and atomic.h (the
  CAS.h functions under a name that cannot clash with cas.h).
- set: redesigned around what the programs use. A set starts empty with no
  memory (SET(), set_init), grows by itself, never aborts when the memory runs
  out (set_add and set_insert say SET_ERROR), and iterates, combines and joins
  in the order of insertion instead of hash order. New: set_first and
  set_last (O(1)) in place of set_at, set_at_n, set_at_sa; set_join_s and
  set_join report failure. Removed because nothing used them: set_union,
  set_sub, set_subtract, set_difference, set_toarray, set_joinq, set_find,
  set_realloc, set_free_array, set_inserts, set_insertsa, fmt_set,
  stralloc_catset, set_iterator_find, set_iterator_erase and the hash argument
  of set_init. set_delete freed a pointer into its own table and crashed when
  the value was missing, set_clear leaked every value, set_at crashed, set_has
  on a new set divided by zero, set_joinq closed quotes it never opened; the
  initial table is 16 slots instead of 1024 (48 kB per set)
- hmap: rewritten. An entry was 1104 bytes with a key of at most 1023 bytes
  that was copied without a check, and every map reserved 1024 of them: a
  JSON object cost 1.1 MB of address space and a document of 20000 small
  objects could not be read. Entries are now allocated one at a time with the
  key after them, the table is made by the first insert and doubles, the size
  is O(1), delete frees the entry. Reading 80000 JSON object keys takes 0.12 s
  instead of 177 s, a flat XML document of 20000 elements 13 ms instead of
  260 ms (and 62 ms instead of 9 s for four times that)
- charbuf: reading from a string (charbuf_froms) measured the length of the
  rest of the string for every byte, so parsing JSON from a string was
  quadratic: 20000 array elements took 1.3 s, now 11 ms
- cbmap: inserting a key that is the start of a key that is in the map (or
  the other way round) read beyond the stored key and could overwrite the
  value of the other key; keys that differ only by zero bytes at the end are
  refused; cbmap_get takes NULL for the value and its length
- utf8: strict decoding (overlong forms, surrogates and values above U+10FFFF
  are invalid, u8_to_wc returns 0 for them, the bulk converters return
  (size_t)-1); u8s_chrs, u8s_rchrs, u8b_chrs, u8b_rchrs looped for ever on an
  invalid byte; u8b_diff ignored the length of the first argument;
  wcs_to_u8sa appended nothing; wcs_to_u8s wrote a byte too many and never
  wrote the entity it counted for characters it cannot encode; u8s_len and
  u8b_len read past a cut off sequence; wcs_to_u8sa is declared (HAVE_STRALLOC_H
  was never defined)
- slist, dlist: slist_find never advanced (endless loop when nothing
  matched), slist_find_delete crashed when nothing matched, slist.h declared
  dlist_find, slist_foreach_safe and slink_foreach_safe never ended,
  dlist_swap broke the list for neighbours, dlist_foreach_down_safe did not
  compile; dlist.h declares find, find_delete, swap, move_head, move_tail
- elf, pe: elf_check and pe_check (a text that says what is wrong, or NULL)
  look at a whole image before anything walks it: headers, tables, sections,
  string tables, symbol and dynamic names, export names, import tables. elflist,
  elf64list, elfwrsec, pelist, pemkwingui, impgen and ntldd call them first, so
  corrupt, truncated and non-ELF/PE files give an error message instead of a
  crash (elflist, elf64list, pelist and four more crashed on every such file)
- buffer: buffer_mmapprivate, buffer_mmapread and their _fd forms leave an
  empty, valid buffer when they fail, as the callers (nine tools) never looked at
  the result and read an uninitialized one; buffer_lzma wrote each round of
  its output over the last and never ended on an error, which corrupted every
  .xz/.lzma file bigger than the buffer and hung ccat on bad input
- array, byte, str: array_allocate grows by half instead of a fixed 4 kB step;
  byte_copy, byte_copyr, byte_zero, byte_fill, byte_chr, str_len and str_chr
  use the C library where the result is the same
- ini, xml, hashmap: ini_get, ini_section and ini_keys accept the NULL section
  of a file that held none (exe-wrap died on it); xml_print and the finders accept
  NULL; json refuses documents nested deeper than 512 levels
- cpp: reading an identifier made no progress on a byte that is not UTF-8
  (cpre never ended on binary input)
- tools: xml2moon needs its argument (it had a path of the author's machine),
  xmlpp, xml2moon, eagle-init-brd, eagle-to-svg, eagle-gen-cmds, eagle-to-circuit
  and opensearch-dump report a file that cannot be read or holds no XML;
  omflist forgot the 0 that ends the arguments of errmsg_warnsys
- docs: doc/review.md, the assessment of every module and program, and
  proposals for the direction of the project; doc/genmakefile-spec.md
- tests: hmap, elf, pe (valgrind finds no invalid access in any of them);
  removed the stray tests/faillog-* files, tests/test_path.exe and the
  expired test key and certificate http.key, http.crt
- tests: utf8, range, slist, dlist, cb, cbmap, set (and more cases for json,
  http, sse); valgrind reports no invalid access over all of them
- doc/http.md: the guide to the HTTP client, its streaming parts and the
  tools built on it; manual pages for every function of http.h (rewritten,
  several described functions that do not exist), the overview http(3),
  buffer_chunked_encode, http_socket_read, http_socket_write, and sse_init,
  sse_free, sse_feed, ndjson_init, ndjson_free, ndjson_feed as pages of
  their own
- json: parsing no longer leaks the key strings of objects and one node per
  document (a streamed answer leaked with every event); http_open frees the
  result of the name lookup
- http: http_free releases the buffers, requests and responses of a
  connection (cgemini and copenai leaked about 48 kB per request);
  http_header refuses names and values with a CR or LF, which could add
  headers; an URL without a path asks for "/" instead of sending an empty
  request target
- http: requests of any method with headers and a body: http_open,
  http_post, http_header, http_body, and a body sent as chunks while it is
  produced: http_chunked, http_chunk, http_chunk_end, http_body_flush; the
  Host header has the port when it is not the default one, the request
  line is HTTP/1.1 for POST and chunks
- http: http_recv decodes the response body (chunked, Content-Length or
  until close) incrementally for streamed answers, set h->stream to leave
  the body in the input buffer; the status code of the response is parsed
  by http_canread; stray "failed recv()" lines on non-blocking reads are gone
- tls: tls_servername sets the SNI name, http_socket announces the host;
  writes may be retried from a buffer that grew or moved
- sse: new module, a parser for server-sent events that accepts any
  pieces, LF, CR LF and CR line ends, comments, id and retry
- json: strings are stored as the text they stand for (\u escapes and
  surrogate pairs decode to UTF-8, an escaped backslash before u00e4 is no
  longer taken for an escape) and are printed with every backslash escaped;
  json_valid checks a text strictly, json_parse_text reads it, only if it
  is valid; json_property_path follows candidates[0].content.parts[0].text;
  ndjson_feed reads newline delimited JSON; exponents with a sign (1e+5)
  are numbers; json_property_get no longer crashes on a missing key
- copenai: new tool, asks an OpenAI compatible chat API with a chunked
  POST and writes the streamed answer (SSE or NDJSON) as it arrives
- cgemini: new tool, conversation with Google Gemini ($GEMINI_API_KEY) on
  the terminal, through a pipe or for the clients of a TCP socket (-l)
- scan: scan_double reads exponents with a sign (1e-5, 2.5E+3), it
  rejected them before
- io: io_sendfile compiles again on Linux (a closing brace was missing)

2026-10-30:

- genmakefile: -t bcc102 selects the clang based bcc32c of Embarcadero C++
  10.2 (C99): its predefined macros are known, so the preprocessor scan
  runs, and the classic bcc32 options and run time libraries are left out;
  programs are linked by calling $(ILINK) (default ilink32) itself, and
  make -DILINK=... names another one (the 6.90 linker does not run under
  wine, the one of bcc55 links the objects); -L directories of a Borland
  make file are written with backslashes
- genmakefile: --library NAME makes one library of the sources without a
  main(), for the flat third party trees; -i then installs NAME.lib and its
  public headers (--header, and those below an include directory) below
  $(prefix) (default inst-<compiler>); .c files can be given as arguments,
  not only directories
- third_party/compat/bcc55/stdint.h: the part of stdint.h Borland C++ 5.5
  lacks
- genmakefile: the packages the sources query as HAVE_BROTLI, HAVE_LIBBZ2,
  HAVE_LIBLZMA and HAVE_ZLIB (and any named with --dep) are enabled from the
  make file by giving LIBBROTLI, LIBBZ2, LIBLZMA, LIBZ (plus _INCLUDEDIR and
  _LIBDIR) a value, and are off otherwise
- genmakefile: a Borland library that already exists is updated member by
  member (tlib -+), so a rebuilt object no longer stays stale in it
- fmt_double: builds with Borland C++ 5.5, which has no isnan and isinf
- genmakefile: a program links the libraries behind a header that only
  includes others (map.h gives hmap), and a library links those of the
  public headers its own sources reach (io.h gives iarray)
- genmakefile: Borland libraries are filled again (tlib reads a - in a bare
  module name as an operator, so every module was dropped as a duplicate);
  -l names a Borland import library as NAME.lib without a lib prefix
- genmakefile: only the names that begin with USE_ (--switch-prefix), that
  no #define of the sources gives a value (fallbacks included) and that no #if
  compares or calculates with become switches in the generated file; the
  conditionals of branches that are not taken are read as well
- genmakefile: the mkdir rules of a Borland make file used mkdir -p, and
  were not made at all for directories named with backslashes, so the
  objects had no directory to go to
- lib/cpp: track_queries also collects the names of the #defines read
  (defined_names) and the names an #if uses as more than a truth value
  (undef_cmp)
- sources for Borland C++ 5.5 (C89): declarations moved to the top of their
  block, no empty initializers or compound literals in lib/cpp, uint16_t,
  uint32_t and uint64_t, alloca and HAVE_ALLOCA in lib/windoze.h, Unix-only
  files stubbed (io_passfd, iob_prefetch), #warning and tls files guarded;
  lib/path: DRIVE() searched with str_contains where str_has was meant;
  PATH_LIST_FOREACH takes the position variable as its fifth argument
- genmakefile: the output, work and object directories are made with the
  directory separator of the system it runs on. For a Borland or nmake file
  the separator of the make program (a backslash) was used, which made
  directories named like \tmp\x\y in the working directory
- lib/io.h: config.h is only included when HAVE_CONFIG_H is defined, like in
  the other files, so that a build without a configure step (Borland make)
  finds all it needs
- genmakefile: a header alone in lib/ (lib/windoze.h) is no library module
  any more, a module needs a directory lib/<name>/ with sources as well
- genmakefile: added --xtal HZ, --baud BPS and --code-offset ADDRESS for
  firmware builds. With --chip and one of them, a file builds one point of
  the matrix of the PIC projects: objects below
  obj/<compiler>-<chip>/<type>_<mhz>mhz_<kbps>kbps[_at<offset>]/<program>/,
  binaries as bin/<compiler>-<chip>/<program>_<type>_<mhz>mhz_<kbps>kbps[_at<offset>],
  and the macros _XTAL_FREQ, XTAL_USED (48 MHz: intosc48), UART_BAUD,
  PIC<CHIP>, __<chip> and __DEBUG or __NDEBUG are defined
- genmakefile: the names the sources ask about in #if conditionals and
  that nothing defines (USE_UART, _XTAL_FREQ and the like, but not the
  compiler macros starting with __) become switches in the generated file:
  a build that is given a value for one, for example make USE_UART=1 (make
  -DUSE_UART=1 for Borland make and nmake), adds -DUSE_UART=1 to DEFS. Without
  a value the name stays undefined. Written for make, gmake, Borland make,
  nmake, batch and shell files; ninja has no way to take them
- lib/cpp: with track_queries set in cpp_ctx the preprocessor collects the
  names a conditional asks about while no macro of that name exists:
  undef_tested for #ifdef, #ifndef and defined(), undef_valued for names
  used by value in an #if or #elif
- genmakefile: the preprocessor scan finds the names the sources query and
  never define (the USE_* switches of the PIC firmware), tested and valued
  separately; -v tells how many, -v -v lists them
- genmakefile: added -v/--verbose, which tells what is being done (the
  compiler and make type, the output file, the scan, the rule creation);
  given twice it also names every source the scan starts with. Headers
  reached while scanning are not listed
- genmakefile: the state dump of a DEBUG_OUTPUT build is now off unless
  -G/--dump is given; once it prints the settings, twice it also prints
  every rule. The debug build no longer writes every rule while outputting
  (about 29000 lines less for the tools of src/)
- genmakefile: moved the compiler and target setup (set_machine, set_chip,
  set_system, set_make_type, set_compiler_type, resolve_toolchain) out of
  genmakefile.c into compiler.c and compiler.h
- genmakefile: sources are scanned with the preprocessor of lib/cpp when
  the predefined macros of the compiler are known (bcc55 so far), so #if
  gates are evaluated for the target. Other compilers keep the text scanner
- genmakefile: programs are the sources with a main(), lib/<name>.h with
  lib/<name>/ is a library module, every program links the modules its
  headers name (and what those need), header dependencies are listed for
  each object
- genmakefile: added --lib-dir to name the directory of the lib modules
- genmakefile: bcc55 builds use .obj, .exe and .lib, link cw32mt.lib and
  import32.lib, and the terminator of an inline file starts its line as
  Borland make requires
- lib/cpp: added a scan-only mode for dependency scans (missing headers and
  #error are skipped, headers below the compiler's include directories are
  not read, the gcc macros of the host are not defined)
- lib/typedefs.h, lib/uint16.h, lib/uint32.h, lib/uint64.h: fixed the type
  definitions for Borland C++ 5.5 (ssize_t, duplicate uint8/16/32 typedefs
  from systypes.h, uint32 on bcc32 versions before 5.7)
- known: genmakefile reports a stray #endif for files that do not end in a
  newline (see cpp-scan-stray-endif in BUGS)
- ini, uri, debug: moved from the project root into the libraries lib/ini,
  lib/uri and lib/debug, one function per file. ini_read now chains the
  sections it reads, ini_write and ini_keys no longer crash
- cache.[ch]: moved to src/dnsutils, only nscache and query use it
- tests: unit tests for ini, uri, textcode, strarray, rdir, binfmt, xml
  (build, print, read, token scanner), json, tokenizer, ihex and bz2
  compression, replacing the test programs they came from
- src: the programs left in the project root moved to src/text, src/system,
  src/filedata, src/tcp, src/toolchain and src/cxx, the root holds no
  source code any more
- removed the programs dnstest (a copy of dnsip), jsontest (jsonpp does the
  job), xmltest, xmltest2, xmlscantest, tokenizertest, cpptest2, binfmttest,
  strarraytest, buffertest, rdir-test, xmltest3, xmltest4, ziptest, preproc.c
- re-enabled macho32list, montty and cpptest in the build
- ccat: compresses as well, run as czip (installed as a link) it compresses
  by default, the type comes from -t or the file name; the default level is
  3 as the usage text says
- buffer_bz2: compressing to bz2 wrote an empty file and lost data at every
  block of 300000 bytes, fixed
- ihextool: the new installed Intel HEX tool with the commands info,
  records, dump, merge (cut, extract), bin, frombin, verify and sum, it
  replaces testihex. lib/ihex gets ihex_range, ihex_copy_to,
  ihex_record_base, ihex_verify, ihex_write_data and ihex_write_eof, the
  reader skips lines that are no record
- ctlscat: the new installed netcat style relay for TLS or, with -n, plain
  TCP, it connects or with -l waits for one connection. It replaces ssltest
- known: gzip compression and decompression through buffer_deflate and
  buffer_inflate corrupt data above 32768 bytes (see BUGS)
- lib/http: buffer_chunked decodes the chunked transfer encoding (it did
  not work before), the new buffer_chunked_encode writes it. tests/test_http.c
- chttpcat: the new installed HTTP and HTTPS client, requests every URL on
  the command line and writes the bodies to standard output. Data on standard
  input is sent as a chunked POST, -i shows the headers, -H adds a header,
  -X sets the method, -L follows redirects. It replaces httptest
- ctlscat and chttpcat share address_connect (src/dnsutils)
- cftpcat: the new installed FTP client, ftp://host/dir/ lists a directory,
  ftp://host/file downloads it, and HOST COMMAND runs ls, nlst, cat, cd, pwd,
  size or quote (several separated by semicolons) on the server, like lftp -e.
  It uses EPSV and falls back to PASV, -u -p log in, -P sets the port, -v
  shows the dialogue. It replaces ftptest
- cmake: Functions.cmake gets check_includes, check_include_def, var2define
  and clean_name from the qjs projects, and check_function_def now also
  defines HAVE_<FUNCTION>=1 for the compiler
- cpre: cpptest is now the installed C preprocessor cpre (-D -I -P -L, dump
  defines, dependencies)
- fixed fmt_double: it rounded -2.25 down to -2.24999, read an uninitialised
  value and printed nothing for large numbers. It now gives the shortest
  form up to prec digits, 1e20 for large and 1.5e-7 for small values, NaN
  and Inf
- fixed fmt_escapecharjson (printable characters came out as \u0041),
  fmt_escapecharnonprintable (wrong length for \n and octal escapes) and
  fmt_escapecharquotedprintable (lower case hex digits, now =AB)
- fixed gzip: buffer_deflate lost data above the buffer size and wrote no
  trailer, so gzip rejected the result, and buffer_inflate never read on
  past the first block. ccat and czip now agree with gzip on files of any
  size. buffer_bz2 had the same read problem and stops at truncated data
- ccat: reports corrupt or truncated input with exit status 1 (it used its
  own buffer_copy that ignored errors), -t alone selects decompression, and
  compressing to a redirected standard output works
- fixed the XML reader (xml_read_tree): elements with attributes no longer
  crash it or lose their attributes, self-closing elements are found, text
  after a child element is kept, comments with a > in them, CDATA and a
  doctype are handled, and attributes may use single quotes, no quotes or
  no value. The cause was buffer_skip_pred (and so buffer_skipspace), which
  also swallowed the first byte that did not match; it now stops in front
  of it. xml_attributes no longer prints a dump of the attributes
- fixed the tokenizer: hex literals such as 0x2a, 0XFF and 0x1UL are typed
  TT_HEX_INT_LIT (they came out as TT_UNKNOWN with result 0). The header now
  says that TT_EOF is an ordinary token that repeats at the end of input
- fixed the JSON printers of the library: json_compact_printer and
  json_default_printer wrote strings below the top level without quotes
  (the output was not valid JSON), the compact one is now free of white
  space, and json_tostring uses it instead of a printer with uninitialised
  fields. Control characters in strings are escaped, and the reader turns
  \n, \r, \t, \b and \f in a string into the character
- lib: cpp_convert_int and http_read use the int64 and uint32 types of the
  library, no more int64_t and uint32_t
- lib: io_receivefd is back (io_passfd could send a descriptor but io.h
  declared a receiver that did not exist) and fmt_8longlong, the octal
  formatter for 64-bit values on systems where long has 32 bits
- fixed io_closeonexec, it set the flag on the wrong fcntl command and the
  descriptor stayed inheritable. io_get_wantread and io_get_wantwrite now
  report what the caller asked for, not the state the kernel still has
